course: french
lesson: 13
title: Weather and seasons
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: French talks about the weather with il fait, "it makes": il fait beau (it's nice), il fait chaud (it's hot), il fait froid (it's cold), il fait mauvais (it's bad).
explain: Some weather has its own little verb: il pleut (it's raining) and il neige (it's snowing); and for things you see in the sky you use il y a, "there is": il y a des nuages (it's cloudy).
explain: Your body is different: when YOU are hot you say j'ai chaud, "I have hot", and when you are cold, j'ai froid.
explain: Picture a Christmas card from Melbourne with a beach on it: France is in the northern half of the world, so July is summer there, while in Australia it is winter.
explain: French has funny weather sayings: a dreadful stormy day is un temps de chien, "a dog's weather", and heavy rain is il pleut des cordes, "it's raining ropes".
fact: Quel temps fait-il ? means "What's the weather like?"; Il pleut ("eel PLUH") means "It's raining".
fact: For weather French uses il fait: il fait beau (it's nice), il fait chaud (it's hot), il fait froid (it's cold), il fait mauvais (it's bad), il fait gris (it's dull and grey).
fact: For how your body feels French uses avoir: j'ai chaud (I'm hot), j'ai froid (I'm cold); je suis chaud means something else in slang, so avoid it.
fact: il y a means "there is" or "there are": il y a des nuages (it's cloudy), il y a du vent (it's windy), il y a du soleil (it's sunny).
fact: Il neige means "It's snowing"; la neige is snow and un bonhomme de neige is a snowman.
fact: un orage is a thunderstorm, le tonnerre is thunder and un éclair is a flash of lightning (and also a chocolate pastry); Il y a de l'orage means there's a storm.
fact: The seasons are le printemps (spring), l'été (summer), l'automne (autumn) and l'hiver (winter).
fact: France is in the northern half of the world, so July is summer and Christmas is in winter there; in Australia and New Zealand it is the other way round.
fact: "In" a season is en été, en automne, en hiver, but au printemps, because printemps starts with a consonant.
fact: France uses degrees Celsius, like the UK: il fait vingt degrés (20 °C) is warm, a T-shirt day; 0 °C is freezing; 20 °C is about 68 °F.
fact: Il fait un temps de chien ! means the weather is awful; Il pleut des cordes ! means it's pouring.
fact: beau means beautiful or handsome, but il fait beau simply means the weather is sunny and nice.
fact: Slang here: "chucking it down" (UK) means raining hard; "dreich" (Scotland) means grey, damp and gloomy; "mental" (Scotland) means crazy; "wicked" (Boston) means very; "mate" means friend.
exercise: How do you say "It's raining"? | Il pleut. | Il est pluie. | Il fait pleut. | Il y a pleut.
exercise: How do you ask "What's the weather like?" | Quel temps fait-il ? | Quelle heure est-il ? | Il fait quel temps, toi ? | Quel temps est-il ?
exercise: How do you say "It's hot" about the weather? | Il fait chaud. | Il est chaud. | J'est chaud. | Il a chaud.
exercise: Cooper is too hot in Phoenix. What should he say about himself? | J'ai chaud. | Je suis chaud. | Il fait moi chaud. | Je fais chaud.
exercise: How do you say "I'm cold"? | J'ai froid. | Je suis froid. | Il fait froid moi. | J'ai froide.
exercise: What does il y a des nuages mean? | it's cloudy | it's snowing | it's windy | it's foggy
exercise: Which phrase means "it's windy"? | il y a du vent | il fait vent | il est venteux | il y a des vents
exercise: Lily's cousin writes "Il neige !" What is happening? | It's snowing | A horse is neighing | It's raining | It's very hot
exercise: What is un orage? | a thunderstorm | an orange | a cloud | a rainbow
exercise: What does un éclair mean in a storm? | a flash of lightning | a clap of thunder | a heavy shower | a strong wind
exercise: Which is summer? | l'été | l'hiver | l'automne | le printemps
exercise: Ruby in Melbourne asks why juillet is summer in France. What's the reason? | France is in the northern half of the world | France is nearer the Sun than Australia | The French count months backwards | French summer lasts all year
exercise: How do you say "in spring"? | au printemps | en printemps | dans printemps | à printemps
exercise: How do you say "in winter"? | en hiver | au hiver | à l'hiver | dans hiver
exercise: The forecast says "Il fait vingt degrés." What should Jayden pack? | A T-shirt | A thick winter coat | A snowsuit | Ski boots
exercise: What does Il fait un temps de chien ! mean? | The weather is awful | It's raining dogs | The dog is hot | It's a good day for dogs
exercise: What does Il fait beau mean? | The weather is nice | The weather is handsome | It's beautiful inside | It's a bow
test: It's chucking it down in Manchester ("chucking it down" means raining hard) and Ellie says "Il est rain." The class laughs. What should she say? | Il pleut. | Il fait pluie. | Il est pleut. | Il y a pleut.
test: Aisha says "Il est chaud" about the weather. Her teacher says, "Close, but..." What's the rule? | Weather uses il fait: il fait chaud | Weather uses j'ai: j'ai chaud | Weather uses il y a: il y a chaud | Weather uses c'est: c'est chaud
test: Cooper's penpal replies "Tu veux dire : j'ai chaud !" Why? | For your body you use avoir: j'ai chaud | For your body you use être: je suis chaud | Only grown-ups can say they are hot | French people never say they are hot
test: Isla in Aberdeen says, "It's pure dreich." ("Dreich" means grey and gloomy.) What would a French person say? | Il fait gris. | Il fait dreich. | Il est nuage. | Il pleut des chiens.
test: Lily asks, "Is that like 'it's neighing'? Is there a horse?" What would you build in the garden if il neige? | un bonhomme de neige, a snowman | un cheval de neige, a snow horse | un château de sable, a sandcastle | un bonhomme de pluie, a rain man
test: Tom asks, "Is orage orange?" during thunder. What's the truth? | No, un orage is a thunderstorm | Yes, orage is the colour orange | No, orage is a flash of lightning | Yes, but only at sunset
test: Ruby says, "July's winter, mate, that's backwards!" Who is right? | Both: July is winter in Australia and summer in France | Ruby: July is winter everywhere | The French: July is summer everywhere | Neither: July is always spring
test: Leo asks, "Why's spring the odd one out?" after writing "en printemps". What's the reason? | printemps starts with a consonant, so it takes au | printemps is feminine, so it takes au | Spring is too short to use en | Leo is right: en printemps is correct
test: Jayden asks, "Is vingt degrés, like, freezing?" What's the truth? | No, 20 °C is warm, a T-shirt day | Yes, 20 degrees is freezing | Yes, it is 20 degrees below zero | No, 20 °C is a heatwave
test: Hannah says, "A dog's weather? That's mental." ("Mental" means crazy.) Which English saying is closest? | raining cats and dogs | it's a dog's life | let sleeping dogs lie | every dog has its day
test: Mason guesses il fait beau means "wicked handsome weather". ("Wicked" means very.) What does it really mean? | The weather is sunny and nice | The weather is good-looking people | A storm is coming | It's a bit cold
test: ★★★ It's July in Paris and in Sydney on the same day. Which pair of sentences fits best? | Paris: il fait chaud. Sydney: il fait froid. | Paris: il fait froid. Sydney: il fait chaud. | Paris: il neige. Sydney: il neige. | Paris: c'est l'hiver. Sydney: c'est l'été.
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression of weather conditions in French? | They are customarily expressed by the construction il fait followed by an adjective | They are customarily expressed by the construction il est followed by an adjective | They are customarily expressed by j'ai followed by a noun | They are invariably expressed in English
exam: Identify the correct expression of the sensation of personal coldness. | J'ai froid | Je suis froid | Il fait moi froid | J'ai froide
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the prepositions employed with the seasons. | En precedes été, automne and hiver, whereas au precedes printemps | En precedes all four seasons without exception | Au precedes all four seasons without exception | Dans precedes été and hiver, whereas à precedes printemps
exam: It is given that the temperature in France is reported as vingt degrés. Determine the most accurate characterisation of the conditions. | Warm, approximately sixty-eight degrees Fahrenheit | Severe frost, twenty degrees below zero | Extreme heat, twenty degrees above boiling | Mild cold, twenty degrees Fahrenheit
